Agent service — design decisions #agents #cloud #design

The cloud Nexus **agent service**: Waldo plus the specialist agents, the runtime
that runs them on a user's nexus, and the Studio that manages them. This file is
the settled design, captured from a voice grill-me session (`scratch/grill-waldo`,
22 decisions). It is **our cloud product** — dogfood workbooks deployed via the
DeployKit, NOT baked into the open-standard nexus image. See [[the-line]].

## Runtime — how an agent runs

- **Unit of execution** — one-shot **function call** from the user's view (a clean
  start/finish), optionally fired by automation (cron). Not a user-managed daemon.
- **Where it runs** — **BEAM by default** (Elixir scheduler does orchestration +
  scheduling). **WASM** only for specific tool/capability logic that wants
  sandboxing or is untrusted/heavy. Agent ≈ [[agent-is-a-sandboxed-function]].
- **Concurrency** — each run is a **separate, supervised BEAM process**; one run
  crashing never takes down another run or the nexus. One nexus per org
  ([[one-nexus-per-org]]); a second wholly-separate nexus is the scale-out unit.
- **Visibility** — runs **stream tokens + lifecycle events onto the #event bus**;
  Studio (and any hook) watches reactively. This is the run-visibility interface.
- **Failure model** — **no blind auto-restart.** An agent validates within its
  `flow`; on a problem it **files an issue** (git/.work model) for later repair by
  a human or another agent. Failure is proactive + declarative, not OTP-restart.

## The agent file — one `.work` `agent` block

- **One block, complete contract** — `prompt + tools + grant + limit + flow`. **No
  separate `server` brain block.** Self-contained identity → logic → caps.
- **Flow** — technically optional (a trivial reactive agent can omit it) but
  **strongly discouraged to omit**: `flow` is the perceive→plan→act loop +
  validation + task management that makes real agency work.
- **Tools** — declared as capability atoms in a **`grant` list**, resolved against
  the **Effects registry** ([[hooks-events-effects-architecture]]) and enforced at
  call time.
- **Limit** — caps **tokens, wall-clock, sub-agent depth, and cost** — but applied
  **sparingly / carefully**: over-tight limits kill legitimate long runs.
- **Default agents** — live in **`dogfood/cloud/agents/`** as `.work` files,
  **seeded into every new nexus**. Users fully own/modify them on disk.
- **Customisation** — users **fork & edit** default agents; **git merge** handles
  upstream updates. No template/instance abstraction.

## Waldo — the manager

- **Remit** — the **expert on the Nexus framework itself**: orchestrator +
  delegator, AND agent-writer + diagnostician + default **workhorse**. He both
  routes work and does it.
- **Singleton** per nexus. **Session memory** lives in a **notes table** in the
  tenant DB (persistent cross-session context).
- **Handoffs** — Waldo **brokers** handoffs between specialists; data passes as
  **ephemeral handoff documents** = `.work` files with file references. Waldo
  stays in control of the orchestration flow.

## Scheduling

- Agents schedule future work via a **`hook` `trigger`** (cron / at / every),
  reusing **Nexus.Scheduler** ([[time-scheduling-lane]]). The **nexus owns** the
  tasks; an agent may schedule for **itself, another agent (with permission), or an
  app**. Ownership is viewable in Studio.
- **Wake behaviour** — overdue tasks generally **fire on wake**, BUT after extended
  downtime do **not** flood the nexus with a backlog. *(Open: define "extended" +
  the backlog policy.)*

## Sub-agents

- **Results are structured** — returned as **`.work`** response/handoff documents,
  not free text. Predictable, robust passing.
- **Studio view** — sub-agent runs are **collapsed under the parent** by default;
  full depth preserved for debugging.

## Issues

- Agent-filed and human-filed issues are **stored the same way** (the .work/git
  model), but the **Studio separates + triages** them with dedicated views. (Note:
  this is the *nexus* issue store — NOT bd, which is local-only/git-ignored.)
  *(Open: exact physical home of the issue store — git-indexed .work vs DB.)*

## Studio — managing it all

- **Starting a task** — both **chat with Waldo** (he delegates) AND **pick a
  specialist directly**. Cater to orchestration-first and know-what-I-want users.
- **HITL approvals** — surface in an **inbox of pending approvals**, asynchronous,
  rather than interrupting the active run. *(Open: whether the inbox grows into a
  full notifications center across approvals / delegated-task status / issues.)*

## Specialist agents

The voice interview degraded before this round, so these are **recommended
decisions** (grounded in repo canon), not yet founder-ratified — flag any you'd
steer differently.

### DeckAgent — presentations via wasm Reveal.js

- **Output** — a **`.work` surface** that renders the deck (a folder with
  `index.work` → mounted at its path, WYSIWYG), **not** a standalone artifact.
  The deck IS a workbook; Reveal.js is the render target of its `client` island.
- **Input** — a **prose brief** (most natural), optionally a structured `design`
  block for tight control. **Iterative** by default: it drafts, you react, it
  revises — same chat-with-preview loop as the Studio split-view.
- **Home** — lands in the active workspace tree (e.g. `<ws>/decks/<name>/`),
  **versioned + committed** like any workspace file (nexus = git remote).

### VideoAgent — via the wasm-video package

- **Job** — **render from a script/storyboard** first (assemble clips + captions +
  audio into a timeline); editing existing footage is a later mode. Input is a
  `.work` storyboard the agent authors/edits.
- **Execution** — rendering is heavy, so it runs as a **scheduled job** (a `flow`
  step), **not** inline in the request. Progress streams onto the **#event bus**
  (Studio shows a progress meter); the finished file lands in the tree + a
  `#event` fires on completion (notify the user).

### ResearchAgent — organized findings, not a doc dump

- **Organising scheme** — by the **active ask / project** (the scope you're working
  in), with a **topic taxonomy inside it** — folders of `.work` notes, cross-linked
  by `[[backlinks]]` + `#tags`. The repo structure mirrors the project, not a flat
  pile.
- **Integrations (v1)** — **web search** + **a small set of MCP tools** (the
  integrations OAuth lane). Arbitrary data sources are post-v1. Keep v1 scope tight:
  search → fetch → organize.
- **Pipelines** — yes: a **scheduled recurring `flow` + `trigger`** (gather →
  dedupe → organize) reusing Nexus.Scheduler — the "research pipeline."
- **Queryability** — primary is **well-named `.work` files + backlinks/tags** (the
  source of truth); an **embedding index is a derived accelerator** layered on top,
  never the store itself. (Pinecone-style index is an optional derived cache.)

## Capabilities, cost, limits

- **Default deny-all** — an agent gets only the grants it declares; resolved vs the
  Effects registry. **Grant escalation requires approval** — surfaces in the same
  Studio approvals inbox as HITL.
- **Cost** — metered **per run, attributed up to per-agent and per-org**. Hitting a
  budget **pauses** the agent (resumable, lands in the approvals inbox) rather than
  hard-failing — matches the "limits applied sparingly" decision.
- **External spend** — agents CAN spend on external APIs (model tokens, search)
  under a **per-org spend cap**, surfaced as an **upsell knob** ([[upsells]]) —
  config-driven via `Nexus.Config`, neutral defaults in the runtime ([[the-line]]).

## Bootstrap & the line

- **Our cloud product, NOT the open standard** — Waldo + the specialists are
  **dogfood workbooks deployed via the DeployKit**, exactly like a customer. The
  open nexus image ships **none** of them. ([[the-line]])
- **Seeding** — when a new org's nexus comes up, the **DeployKit seeds**
  `dogfood/cloud/agents/*.work` into the tree (committed, so they're forkable);
  the dashboard does not special-case them.
- **v1 'done' bar** — **Waldo + ONE specialist (DeckAgent) running a real task
  end-to-end** through the Studio (start → stream → result in tree), with the
  runtime + agent-file contract + issue-filing in place. The other two specialists
  + full scheduling/sub-agent UI follow once that spine is proven.

## Task management & planning (the orchestration backbone)

The heart of orchestration: Waldo and the specialists don't just answer — they
**plan work, decompose it, file tasks, assign them, and dispatch them as runs.**

- **`resource Task`** (tenant SQLite, our cloud store): `tid, title, detail, status
  (open/in_progress/blocked/done/cancelled), agent (assignee), parent (plan tree),
  priority, created_by, run (the rid once dispatched), created, updated`.
- **Plan = a parent task + child subtasks** (`parent` link). Decomposition is just
  creating a parent and its children — no separate "plan" entity.
- **Plan-with-Waldo** — give a goal; Waldo (who knows the specialists) decomposes it
  into assigned subtasks. AI planning without re-plumbing the runtime.
- **Dispatch** — assigning a task to an agent launches a **Run** (reuses the Phase-1
  `do_run` engine), links the `rid` onto the task, and tracks status
  (open→in_progress→done/blocked). The run ledger and the task board share one spine.
- **Generic mechanism vs our product** (THE LINE): a generic **`task` effect** + a
  **`tasks` hook** persist task events to the store — exactly the Event/`log`
  pattern. The bus + effect engine are the open standard; the Task store + Studio
  **Tasks board** (kanban by status) are our cloud product.
- **Scheduling ties in** — a task can carry a `hook trigger` to run on a schedule
  (Phase-2 scheduling, wb-ps2x), and a failed run files an **issue** (wb-35ma) that
  is itself a task. Tasks, runs, schedules, and issues are one connected fabric.

## Issue/task storage — RESOLVED: git-backed `.work` + derived index

Decided (the jj discussion): tasks and issues are **committed `.work` files**, the
**source of truth lives in git**, and the SQLite store becomes a **derived index**.

- **On disk:** `<workspace>/.nexus/tasks/<tid>.work` and `<workspace>/.nexus/issues/<tid>.work`
  — **per-workspace**, so each task/issue travels + versions with the workspace it's
  about (clone, diff in PRs, any git tooling).
- **Truth = git** via the existing `Nexus.Git.commit_file` (bare-repo) path — the same
  mechanism `file_save` uses. The Phase-2 SQLite `resource Task` is kept as a
  **write-through derived index** (fast board reads), rebuildable from disk. This is
  the doc's own pattern: `.work` is truth, indexes are derived accelerators.
- **jj is the intent, but currently DORMANT.** `Nexus.JJ` (colocate + op-log + undo)
  is built + tested but **not wired into any commit path** — and its colocated-`.git`
  model conflicts with the bare-repo topology. So tasks-in-git ships first (proven
  path); **wiring jj into the commit path is a separate piece** (jj is dormant for
  ALL commits, not just tasks). Once jj is live, every workspace + task/issue edit is
  in the op-log → `jj undo` over agent operations. (Issues: git-backed refactor +
  jj-wiring tracked in bd.)
- **josh** (subtree proxy) is NOT in the repo — not a current integration point.

## josh — investigated, RESOLVED: don't adopt now (use native `git subtree`)

Two deep investigations (incl. the wasm-sandbox angle) settled this:

- **josh cannot run in our wasm sandboxes.** josh-core hard-depends on `git2`
  (vendored libgit2, **C**) + `sled` (mmap/atomics embedded DB) — neither builds on
  our `wasm32-wasi` Rust lane (mrustc + libstd, no C build scripts), and our sandbox
  ABI deliberately exposes no git-object-store capability to guests. "Compile the
  concept to wasm" = a multi-month fork off libgit2 onto an unfinished gitoxide-wasm
  target + replacing sled — exactly the tech-debt to avoid. josh's real (only) home
  is a **native sidecar** on the bare repos, never a wasm guest.
- **Subtree composition survives** — josh's `:/`, `:prefix=`, `:[…]`, `:workspace=`
  are bidirectional/reversible and nest; `:workspace=` literally mirrors our
  `workspaces="<subtree>|Name|emoji"`. So the concern ("only vital if composition
  survives") is satisfied — but only in the sidecar form.
- **We already have** IDE-sync + GitHub-monorepo mirror from `Nexus.Git`/`GitHttp`.
  The only gap josh closes is **bidirectional subtree views across workspaces**.
- **Decision:** close that gap with the **native `git` we already shell to** —
  incremental cached `git subtree split` for read views + subtree push-back — NOT a
  new Rust sidecar. Adopt **josh-as-native-sidecar only if** `git subtree` measurably
  falls short at real volume. **Never** pursue josh→wasm.

## jj attribution + multi-user/tenant undo (the recorded log, not a button)

The point of jj-as-substrate isn't an Undo button — it's a **recorded, attributed
log of changes** (like git log), surfaced where people already look: **Activity**.

- **Attribution.** Every internal commit stamps the AUTHOR (the user or agent who
  made the change) distinct from the committer (the engine). `commit_change`/
  `commit_file` take `author: "name <email>"`; the dashboard passes the signed-in
  user, agent edits will pass the agent name. So both the git log and the jj op-log
  answer "who did this" — the prerequisite for any multi-user scoping.
- **The log lives in Activity.** Each internal commit emits a `workspace.change`
  `#event` (actor, target=sha, tag `change`) → the `log` effect persists it to
  `resource Event` → the Activity feed's new **Changes** tab. This is the
  tenant-scoped, attributed change-log — you don't go spelunking a per-workspace jj
  op-log to see what happened. (The jj op-log stays the per-workspace *undo* ledger;
  Activity is the cross-workspace *record*.)
- **Multi-tenant.** Workspaces are per-tenant (one nexus per org), so both the op-log
  (per working dir on WB_DATA) and the Activity Event rows (tenant-scoped) are
  naturally isolated — a tenant never sees another's changes.
- **Multi-user undo semantics.** Undo is **scoped by attribution**: you may undo
  operations authored by *you or your agents*, not other users'. Mechanically: the
  op-log is filtered by author; `jj undo` (latest op) is the easy case. Undoing a
  *non-latest* op (someone else committed after you) is a `jj op restore`/revert and
  can conflict — that's the v2 hard case, surfaced as a conflict to resolve, never a
  silent clobber of a teammate's later work. v1: attributed log + latest-op undo.

## Agent git workflow — configurable, jj-FIFO default (the GitHub/PR story)

How agent work becomes commits/PRs and round-trips with GitHub. **Decided** + framed.

### Issues ↔ GitHub (DECIDED)
- **PRs are the external artifact**; nexus issues stay **internal** orchestration/triage
  by default (so the failure-model's auto-filed issues never spam GitHub). A task can
  **opt-in to "promote to GitHub Issue"** with bidirectional status sync when it's real
  external work. We already have the GitHub OAuth token (integrations lane) + a one-way
  code mirror; the PR/issue **API client + fetch-back** are the pieces to build.

### The default is the easiest thing: jj FIFO + undo (CRDT-like)
The simplest model — and the recommended default — is **no PR gate at all**: agents
just run, their edits serialize through **jj** first-in-first-out, and the **op-log /
`jj undo` to a point in time** is the safety net. Concurrent agents become jj's
conflict-tolerant merge model — effectively a **CRDT between agents**, which is easier
to manage than locks/gates. This is mostly buildable on what exists (jj substrate +
attribution + undo); the one missing prerequisite is **agents writing to a workspace
branch** (today they run in a VFS sandbox and don't commit to the tree — close this
first; it's the foundation for everything below).

### The configuration layer (per-subtree, declared in `.work`)
On top of the simple default sits an **opt-in workflow config**, declared **per
monorepo subtree/workspace** (this is the monorepo payoff: git semantics per part are
well understood, so policy is per part). Knobs, each built from existing primitives:
- **Review policy** — a reviewer/merger **agent on a PR `hook`**: fire on `pr.opened`,
  route by author (agent-PR → a "merger" reviewer agent; human-PR → a different rule;
  or "Waldo fields all PRs"). The bus + hook + agent run already exist.
- **Branch policy** — e.g. a **prod vs dev** branch per subtree: agents write to `dev`
  by default; writing to `prod` requires explicit design (a gate), so you can't have
  agents constantly pushing production unless you opted in.
- **Validation** — `flow` + `check` units run as CI/CD **before merge**, per subtree.
- Spectrum: from "just jj FIFO + undo, move fast" → "PR + reviewer-agent + prod gate +
  validation flows" for production, chosen per workspace. Same config mechanism either
  end; you dial it up only where you need it.

### Built on (no new engines)
`hook` (PR event → reviewer agent) · `flow`/`check` (validation) · jj (substrate /
undo / CRDT default) · native `git subtree` per-part config (the [[josh]] decision) ·
the `#event` bus (PR opened → hook) · integrations GitHub token (API + fetch-back).

**Open / to decide later:** the exact review-gate config schema (the founder is still
forming it); branch-policy declaration syntax; whether the reviewer is a default agent
(a "merger") shipped with Waldo/deck/research or purely user-configured.

## Still open (genuinely undecided — flag for next pass)

- Issue-store physical home: git-indexed `.work` vs DB table (`wb-35ma`).
- Wake backlog policy after extended downtime (define "extended").
- Whether the approvals inbox grows into a full notifications center.
- Cross-agent scheduling permission model details.
